===================================================
    
Case 1: an empty OVER clause -- ROW_NUMBER counts the row the offset skips     

===================================================
r    c1    c2    
2     1     20     
3     2     30     
4     2     40     

===================================================
    
Case 2: PARTITION BY c1 ORDER BY c2 -- offset 1 returns a row in the partition of the skipped row, offset 2 a row that starts the next partition (a control: the same result before the fix)     

===================================================
r    c1    c2    
2     1     20     
1     2     30     
2     2     40     

===================================================
r    c1    c2    
1     2     30     
2     2     40     

===================================================
    
Case 3: RANK over c1 -- the first returned row is a peer of the skipped row, and the next rows rank after both     

===================================================
r    c1    c2    
1     1     20     
3     2     30     
3     2     40     

===================================================
    
Case 4: ROWNUM in the select list -- each row keeps its ROWNUM while ROW_NUMBER counts the skipped row     

===================================================
rn    r    c1    c2    
2     2     1     20     
3     3     2     30     
4     4     2     40     

===================================================
    
Case 5: a nested-loop join driven by the index scan -- the join loop counts the row the offset skips     

===================================================
r    c1    c2    
2     1     20     
1     2     30     
2     2     40     

===================================================
    
Case 6: FIRST_VALUE over PARTITION BY c1 ORDER BY c2 -- the first value of the partition is the value of the row the offset skips     

===================================================
f    c1    c2    
10     1     20     
30     2     30     
30     2     40     

===================================================
    
Case 7: DENSE_RANK over c1 -- the offset skips a whole rank, and the first returned row takes the next rank     

===================================================
d    c1    c2    
2     2     30     
2     2     40     

===================================================
    
Case 8: a correlated subquery -- each run of the subquery starts its count again and counts the row its offset skips     

===================================================
c1    c2    s    
1     10     2     
1     20     2     
2     30     2     
2     40     2     

===================================================
    
Case 9: a condition on c3, which the index does not hold -- the offset does not become a key limit, and RANK still compares the first returned row with the skipped row     

===================================================
r    c1    c2    
1     1     20     
3     2     30     
3     2     40     

